---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/paths';
---

<Base
  title="tmux Workflow for AI Coding Agents - Forager"
  description="Use Forager to manage tmux coding sessions, reattach while the host stays running, and inspect agent status estimates."
>
  <Nav />

  <article class="pt-32 pb-16 px-6">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose-content">
      <header class="mb-12">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-white mb-6 leading-tight">tmux Workflow for AI Coding Agents</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
          tmux is the terminal multiplexer that makes persistent, multi-session workflows possible. Forager builds on tmux to add a visual dashboard, status detection, lifecycle management, and evidence-oriented review surfaces for AI agent sessions.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>Why tmux Is the Foundation</h2>
        <p>
          Coding agents often run for longer than an SSH connection or terminal window stays open. tmux can keep their processes running after a client disconnects, while the host and tmux server remain up. A reboot ends those processes.
        </p>
        <p>
          tmux also handles windowing, splitting, and session management natively. It is the standard tool for this kind of work, with decades of stability and a minimal resource footprint.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The Single-Window Workflow</h2>
        <p>
          <a href={sitePath('/')}>Forager</a> organizes your tmux sessions into two views:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Agent view</strong>: The TUI dashboard showing all sessions, their status, and metadata. Navigate with keyboard shortcuts, add or remove sessions, and jump into any agent.</li>
          <li><strong>Terminal view</strong>: The actual tmux pane where the AI agent is running. When you select a session in the dashboard and press enter, you drop into the agent's terminal.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Switching between views is a single keypress. You spend most of your time in the dashboard, dipping into individual agent sessions when they need attention.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Keyboard Shortcuts</h2>
        <p>
          Forager is keyboard-driven. The core shortcuts:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>j</code> / <code>k</code> or arrow keys to navigate sessions</li>
          <li><code>Enter</code> to attach to a session</li>
          <li><code>n</code> to add a new session</li>
          <li><code>d</code> to delete a session</li>
          <li><code>g</code> to manage groups</li>
          <li><code>/</code> to search sessions</li>
          <li><code>?</code> to view all shortcuts</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The interface follows vim-style conventions, so the keybindings feel natural to terminal users. All actions are available without reaching for the mouse.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Session Persistence</h2>
        <p>
          While the host and tmux server stay running, sessions can continue after:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Closing the Forager TUI</li>
          <li>Closing your terminal emulator</li>
          <li>SSH disconnects</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Reopen <code>forager</code> to reattach to surviving sessions. Background execution does not guarantee task completion; inspect the terminal, logs and results. A host reboot or tmux server exit ends the live sessions.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>The TUI Layer on Top of tmux</h2>
        <p>
          Raw tmux is powerful but requires memorizing session names and switching commands. Forager adds a visual layer:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Dashboard view</strong> with periodically refreshed status estimates</li>
          <li><strong>Session groups</strong> for organization</li>
          <li><strong>Status detection</strong> based on process and terminal patterns; inspect the session to confirm its state</li>
          <li><strong>Integrated lifecycle</strong> for <a href={sitePath('/guides/git-worktrees-ai-development/')}>git worktrees</a></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          You get the reliability of tmux with the usability of a purpose-built interface.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Getting Started</h2>
        <p>
          You need tmux installed on your system. Most Linux distributions include it in their package managers, and on macOS you can install it via Homebrew:
        </p>
        <pre><code>brew install tmux    # macOS
apt install tmux     # Debian/Ubuntu</code></pre>
        <p>
          Then install and run Forager:
        </p>
        <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kimyoungjin06/forager-cli/main/scripts/install.sh | bash
forager</code></pre>
        <p>
          See the <a href={sitePath('/docs/')}>documentation</a> for the full setup guide. Related guides:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href={sitePath('/guides/manage-ai-coding-agents/')}>Managing multiple AI coding agents</a></li>
          <li><a href={sitePath('/guides/git-worktrees-ai-development/')}>Git worktrees for parallel development</a></li>
          <li><a href={sitePath('/docs/hosted-harness-agents.html')}>Hosted harness agents</a></li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>

  <Footer />
</Base>
